and then, after they leave,

567
00:18:07,070 --> 00:18:09,270
they just release the lock, and that's it.

568
00:18:09,270 --> 00:18:12,050
So with the lock added, let's see how this achieves

569
00:18:12,050 --> 00:18:15,250
the synchronization effect.

570
00:18:15,250 --> 00:18:17,050
As we mentioned earlier, synchronization means

571
00:18:17,050 --> 00:18:18,370
making another task

572
00:18:18,370 --> 00:18:19,690
wait.

573
00:18:19,690 --> 00:18:21,310
Remember this key point:

574
00:18:21,310 --> 00:18:23,210
you make another task wait.

575
00:18:23,210 --> 00:18:24,930
Waiting

576
00:18:24,930 --> 00:18:26,590
can take many forms.

577
00:18:26,590 --> 00:18:27,210
It can be sleeping,

578
00:18:27,210 --> 00:18:28,550
or it can be non-sleeping.

579
00:18:28,550 --> 00:18:29,390
Our focus

580
00:18:29,390 --> 00:18:31,510
will be on the non-sleeping approach as our example.

581
00:18:31,510 --> 00:18:33,170
Okay.

582
00:18:33,170 --> 00:18:35,830
Now suppose the control flow on the left,

583
00:18:35,830 --> 00:18:36,830
the task on the left,

584
00:18:36,830 --> 00:18:39,730
first gets the CPU's execution rights.

585
00:18:39,730 --> 00:18:40,570
So the PC,

586
00:18:40,570 --> 00:18:42,010
first points to this execution flow on the left.

587
00:18:42,010 --> 00:18:43,630
And the PC,

588
00:18:43,630 --> 00:18:45,670
according to our current logic,

589
00:18:45,670 --> 00:18:46,630
as we know, the PC,

590
00:18:46,630 --> 00:18:48,270
doesn't start working right away.

591
00:18:48,270 --> 00:18:50,490
First it has to try to acquire this lock.

592
00:18:50,490 --> 00:18:52,910
Then, after acquire is executed,

593
00:18:52,910 --> 00:18:54,330
since this lock right now,

594
00:18:54,330 --> 00:18:55,450
is in an open state,

595
00:18:55,450 --> 00:18:56,370
that is, a free state,

596
00:18:56,370 --> 00:18:58,690
so when our task on the left,

597
00:18:58,690 --> 00:18:59,410
goes and does the acquire,

598
00:18:59,410 --> 00:19:00,230
as you can imagine,

599
00:19:00,230 --> 00:19:02,690
the state of this lock will naturally change.

600
00:19:02,690 --> 00:19:05,230
It will become the locked state.

601
00:19:05,230 --> 00:19:06,610
It's like this door has just been closed.

602
00:19:06,610 --> 00:19:06,830
Right?

603
00:19:06,830 --> 00:19:09,850
So the task on the left,

604
00:19:09,850 --> 00:19:12,270
can now go ahead and execute its steps with confidence.

605
00:19:12,270 --> 00:19:14,410
The PC just starts moving downward as it executes.

606
00:19:14,410 --> 00:19:17,730
Now, during the execution,

607
00:19:17,730 --> 00:19:18,850
as you can imagine,

608
00:19:18,850 --> 00:19:21,710
suppose we are still in this single-processor multitasking scenario,

609
00:19:21,710 --> 00:19:23,290
then an interrupt could happen.

610
00:19:23,290 --> 00:19:24,850
So what does it mean when an interrupt occurs?

611
00:19:24,850 --> 00:19:26,930
It means our task is going to be switched out.

612
00:19:26,930 --> 00:19:27,410
Right?

613
00:19:27,410 --> 00:19:29,530
So once a switch happens,

614
00:19:29,530 --> 00:19:32,990
as you can imagine, this PC pointer will go from the left side,

615
00:19:32,990 --> 00:19:34,250
the gray part.

616
00:19:34,250 --> 00:19:35,270
The gray color here means,

617
00:19:35,270 --> 00:19:39,090
it's basically like the multitasking we did earlier.

618
00:19:39,090 --> 00:19:41,450
As you know, at this point the PC effectively remembers,

619
00:19:41,450 --> 00:19:42,670
the position to return to.

620
00:19:42,670 --> 00:19:43,830
It remembers the return position.

621
00:19:43,830 --> 00:19:47,870
So once this interrupt happens,

622
00:19:47,870 --> 00:19:50,850
the task on the left essentially leaves the CPU,

623
00:19:50,850 --> 00:19:53,290
and the task on the right gets the CPU.

624
00:19:53,290 --> 00:19:54,830
Now, for the task on the right,

625
00:19:54,830 --> 00:19:56,870
once its control flow gets the CPU,

626
00:19:56,870 --> 00:19:58,930
you know that the very first operation it's going to perform

627
00:19:58,930 --> 00:20:01,010
is definitely to try to acquire this lock,

628
00:20:01,010 --> 00:20:01,410
right?

629
00:20:01,410 --> 00:20:04,110
Now at this point, as you know,

630
00:20:04,110 --> 00:20:06,370
since the current state of the lock is

631
00:20:06,370 --> 00:20:07,730
locked,

632
00:20:07,730 --> 00:20:10,050
you can imagine that its attempt to acquire,

633
00:20:10,050 --> 00:20:13,290
to take the lock, is definitely going to fail.

634
00:20:13,290 --> 00:20:15,230
So since it fails,

635
00:20:15,230 --> 00:20:16,270
the effect we want to achieve,

636
00:20:16,270 --> 00:20:18,470
is to make the task on the right

637
00:20:18,470 --> 00:20:19,310
wait,

638
00:20:19,310 --> 00:20:20,390
and not proceed any further.

639
00:20:20,390 --> 00:20:21,530
Note carefully,

640
00:20:21,530 --> 00:20:22,510
that is to say, what we mean by waiting

641
00:20:22,510 --> 00:20:24,070
In fact, it means it can't proceed further.

642
00:20:24,070 --> 00:20:25,190
Because once it does execute,

643
00:20:25,190 --> 00:20:29,490
then those two execution flows would be running concurrently,

644
00:20:29,490 --> 00:20:31,230
which would cause conflicts.

645
00:20:31,230 --> 00:20:32,610
Now, as for this,

646
00:20:32,610 --> 00:20:33,750
this so-called making it wait,

647
00:20:33,750 --> 00:20:34,870
exactly what mechanism is used

648
00:20:34,870 --> 00:20:35,630
to make it wait?

649
00:20:35,630 --> 00:20:37,270
Well, there are many ways to do it.

650
00:20:37,270 --> 00:20:39,430
For now, let's set that aside.

651
00:20:39,430 --> 00:20:40,790
At least we know,

652
00:20:40,790 --> 00:20:42,810
that is, at this general conceptual level,

653
00:20:42,810 --> 00:20:44,470
the task on the right basically means,

654
00:20:44,470 --> 00:20:45,710
it can no longer move forward.

655
00:20:45,710 --> 00:20:49,450
So, since the task on the right

656
00:20:49,450 --> 00:20:50,350
can't move forward,

657
00:20:50,350 --> 00:20:51,130
then as you all know,

658
00:20:51,130 --> 00:20:55,360
what our entire CPU does

659
00:20:55,360 --> 00:20:56,940
is still to keep going forward.

660
00:20:56,940 --> 00:20:58,800
The CPU is still executing instructions one by one.

661
00:20:58,800 --> 00:21:00,700
But it's like the instruction on the right here

662
00:21:00,700 --> 00:21:03,060
it will actually be executed repeatedly

663
00:21:03,060 --> 00:21:05,020
which is exactly what we'll talk about later,

664
00:21:05,020 --> 00:21:05,980
this spinlock,

665
00:21:05,980 --> 00:21:07,700
which is really designed to achieve this effect

666
00:21:07,700 --> 00:21:09,400
Now, let's not worry about that

667
00:21:09,400 --> 00:21:10,740
let's just say the task on the right

668
00:21:10,740 --> 00:21:11,300
is basically, well,

669
00:21:11,300 --> 00:21:12,440
as you know, it definitely can't keep running

670
00:21:12,440 --> 00:21:13,520
it might be sleeping,

671
00:21:13,520 --> 00:21:15,320
it has left the scheduler

672
00:21:15,320 --> 00:21:20,480
Then naturally, after the new interrupt happens,

673
00:21:20,480 --> 00:21:22,000
our scheduling

674
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:23,060
might come back around again

675
00:21:23,060 --> 00:21:24,220
What I mean by coming back

676
00:21:24,220 --> 00:21:25,600
is that the task on the left

677
00:21:25,600 --> 00:21:28,460
may once again get permission to execute on the CPU

678
00:21:28,460 --> 00:21:30,220
and then it starts running again

679
00:21:30,220 --> 00:21:30,980
Alright?

680
00:21:30,980 --> 00:21:32,300
and it just continues executing from where it was

681
00:21:32,300 --> 00:21:33,580
So as you can see at this point,

682
00:21:33,580 --> 00:21:36,040
its so-called non-preemptible

683
00:21:36,040 --> 00:21:39,600
the instructions inside its critical section,

684
00:21:39,600 --> 00:21:41,280
even though we know the task on the right

685
00:21:41,280 --> 00:21:42,520
will actually still come and preempt it,

686
00:21:42,520 --> 00:21:44,480
but you know, even though it preempts,

687
00:21:44,480 --> 00:21:46,480
it won't actually

688
00:21:46,480 --> 00:21:48,160
because the task on the right won't continue executing,

689
00:21:48,160 --> 00:21:50,020
so it really won't interfere with it.

690
00:21:50,020 --> 00:21:52,580
So the instructions of the task on the left, in effect,

691
00:21:52,580 --> 00:21:55,960
can proceed without interference, logically speaking.

692
00:21:55,960 --> 00:21:58,160
Please take note of this — it's especially important.

693
00:21:58,160 --> 00:22:02,400
Now there's another point to keep in mind:

694
00:22:02,400 --> 00:22:07,180
it's possible that because interrupts keep happening,

695
00:22:07,180 --> 00:22:10,360
so during the execution of the task on the left,

696
00:22:10,360 --> 00:22:12,360
the task on its right

697
00:22:12,360 --> 00:22:14,100
may still preempt it again,

698
00:22:14,100 --> 00:22:16,780
this handling privilege.

699
00:22:16,780 --> 00:22:17,640
So this,

700
00:22:17,640 --> 00:22:19,760
so when we talk about

701
00:22:19,760 --> 00:22:21,200
So this critical section on the left

702
00:22:21,200 --> 00:22:22,740
not being able to run without being interrupted,

703
00:22:22,740 --> 00:22:23,620
that does not mean

704
00:22:23,620 --> 00:22:24,680
the task on the right

705
00:22:24,680 --> 00:22:25,820
won't take part in scheduling.

706
00:22:25,820 --> 00:22:26,740
Instead, it may still

707
00:22:26,740 --> 00:22:27,820
because as long as it doesn't sleep,

708
00:22:27,820 --> 00:22:29,240
it will still be scheduled in.

709
00:22:29,240 --> 00:22:30,240
So this

710
00:22:30,240 --> 00:22:32,220
task on the right

711
00:22:32,220 --> 00:22:35,360
will still preempt the CPU,

712
00:22:35,360 --> 00:22:36,120
and execute.

713
00:22:36,120 --> 00:22:37,540
But as you can imagine,

714
00:22:37,540 --> 00:22:38,120
at this point

715
00:22:38,120 --> 00:22:40,020
it still can't make any further progress,

716
00:22:40,020 --> 00:22:40,960
it can't make any progress.

717
00:22:40,960 --> 00:22:42,100
And that achieves our

718
00:22:42,100 --> 00:22:44,440
synchronization effect here,

719
00:22:44,440 --> 00:22:45,580
keeping the task on the right

720
00:22:45,580 --> 00:22:47,180
that is, equivalent to saying

721
00:22:47,180 --> 00:22:48,980
he's going to be waiting here

722
00:22:48,980 --> 00:22:50,160
waiting only on the lock

723
00:22:50,160 --> 00:22:51,300
It's like a person outside a door

724
00:22:51,300 --> 00:22:52,480
he just has to wait; he can't get in

725
00:22:52,480 --> 00:22:55,380
So, on this point,

726
00:22:55,380 --> 00:22:56,760
I hope everyone, when understanding

727
00:22:56,760 --> 00:22:59,000
this synchronization and this

728
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:01,580
concurrency, synchronization and locks,

729
00:23:01,580 --> 00:23:02,600
will keep this in mind.

730
00:23:02,600 --> 00:23:04,140
Right.

731
00:23:04,140 --> 00:23:05,600
So as you can imagine,

732
00:23:05,600 --> 00:23:06,960
in short,

733
00:23:06,960 --> 00:23:07,700
the task on the right,

734
00:23:07,700 --> 00:23:09,020
since it has no chance

735
00:23:09,020 --> 00:23:11,600
to execute the code in its critical section,

736
00:23:11,600 --> 00:23:12,820
then the task on the left,

737
00:23:12,820 --> 00:23:14,480
the one on the left,

738
00:23:14,480 --> 00:23:15,940
even though it may be preempted,

739
00:23:15,940 --> 00:23:18,760
it can still, logically,

740
00:23:18,760 --> 00:23:20,340
guarantee that its...

741
00:23:20,340 --> 00:23:22,520
The instructions in the critical section

742
00:23:22,520 --> 00:23:24,040
can be safely

743
00:23:24,040 --> 00:23:25,380
logically safe

744
00:23:25,380 --> 00:23:26,360
will be

745
00:23:26,360 --> 00:23:29,820
when we say it is logically safe

746
00:23:29,820 --> 00:23:32,140
logically it can still be interrupted in practice

747
00:23:32,140 --> 00:23:32,800
please note this

748
00:23:32,800 --> 00:23:34,300
in practice it can still be interrupted

749
00:23:34,300 --> 00:23:36,380
so it is logically safe

750
00:23:36,380 --> 00:23:37,660
running all the way to the end

751
00:23:37,660 --> 00:23:39,660
but in between there can still be interruptions

752
00:23:39,660 --> 00:23:41,480
this is something you also need to pay attention to

753
00:23:41,480 --> 00:23:43,840
when understanding this multitasking

754
00:23:43,840 --> 00:23:46,440
so this, so finally

755
00:23:46,440 --> 00:23:49,480
because there is a release process

756
00:23:49,480 --> 00:23:50,720
the release process

757
00:23:50,720 --> 00:23:53,040
so as you can imagine

758
00:23:53,040 --> 00:23:54,680
once it is released

759
00:23:54,680 --> 00:23:57,760
then this lock

760
00:23:57,760 --> 00:23:59,600
will naturally be released

761
00:23:59,600 --> 00:24:01,020
Finally,

762
00:24:01,020 --> 00:24:02,900
this process of ours will still be released.

763
00:24:02,900 --> 00:24:04,380
So once it is released,

764
00:24:04,380 --> 00:24:05,520
then this lock of ours,

765
00:24:05,520 --> 00:24:07,860
when the control flow on the right is executing,

766
00:24:07,860 --> 00:24:08,780
it will see that this lock

767
00:24:08,780 --> 00:24:11,080
is effectively in the unlocked state.

768
00:24:11,080 --> 00:24:12,400
So at this point, as you know,

769
00:24:12,400 --> 00:24:14,040
once an interrupt occurs,

770
00:24:14,040 --> 00:24:16,160
and the task on the right gets control,

771
00:24:16,160 --> 00:24:17,960
its acquired operation

772
00:24:17,960 --> 00:24:19,520
will actually succeed,

773
00:24:19,520 --> 00:24:20,400
unlike before,

774
00:24:20,400 --> 00:24:21,660
when it would be blocked here.

775
00:24:21,660 --> 00:24:23,940
So at this point,

776
00:24:23,940 --> 00:24:26,300
it is as if the task on the right

777
00:24:26,300 --> 00:24:27,460
has acquired this lock again.

778
00:24:27,460 --> 00:24:28,500
After acquiring this lock,

779
00:24:28,500 --> 00:24:31,500
it can continue executing sequentially,

780
00:24:31,500 --> 00:24:35,220
executing all the way until the execution on its right

781
00:24:35,220 --> 00:24:36,100
executes successfully

782
00:24:36,100 --> 00:24:37,400
So this entire process

783
00:24:37,400 --> 00:24:38,320
is actually

784
00:24:38,320 --> 00:24:39,700
as you can see,

785
00:24:39,700 --> 00:24:41,300
throughout this whole process,

786
00:24:41,300 --> 00:24:42,800
the two tasks achieve

787
00:24:42,800 --> 00:24:45,060
this synchronization effect.

788
00:24:45,060 --> 00:24:47,580
This is what we mean by

789
00:24:47,580 --> 00:24:49,660
introducing locks

790
00:24:49,660 --> 00:24:50,940
to achieve synchronization,

791
00:24:50,940 --> 00:24:51,900
the internal

792
00:24:51,900 --> 00:24:53,880
low-level implementation process.

793
00:24:53,880 --> 00:24:58,100
Now,

794
00:24:58,100 --> 00:25:00,380
after introducing this concept of locks,

795
00:25:00,380 --> 00:25:01,920
when you program from now on,

796
00:25:01,920 --> 00:25:03,060
you will probably

797
00:25:03,060 --> 00:25:04,660
need to pay special attention to certain issues.

798
00:25:04,660 --> 00:25:05,720
For example,

799
00:25:05,720 --> 00:25:10,380
for example, we often run into what is called the deadlock problem.

800
00:25:10,380 --> 00:25:11,900
Deadlock is called deadlock

801
00:25:11,900 --> 00:25:14,160
So what exactly is a deadlock?

802
00:25:14,160 --> 00:25:16,140
Here's the basic definition.

803
00:25:16,140 --> 00:25:19,280
When, along the execution path of a control flow,

804
00:25:19,280 --> 00:25:21,280
multiple locks are involved,

805
00:25:21,280 --> 00:25:23,700
the example we just solved was still fairly simple,

806
00:25:23,700 --> 00:25:24,600
with just one lock, for instance.

807
00:25:24,600 --> 00:25:28,440
But as our logic grows more and more complex,

808
00:25:28,440 --> 00:25:30,240
there will be more and more cases of synchronization.

809
00:25:30,240 --> 00:25:32,720
Under these more complicated situations,

810
00:25:32,720 --> 00:25:35,040
we may need to define multiple locks,

811
00:25:35,040 --> 00:25:39,420
to synchronize different shared resources and critical sections.

812
00:25:39,420 --> 00:25:41,120
So at this point,

813
00:25:41,120 --> 00:25:42,940
what we need to pay attention to is,

814
00:25:42,940 --> 00:25:44,240
a deadlock definitely occurs

815
00:25:44,240 --> 00:25:45,860
in situations where multiple locks exist.

816
00:25:45,860 --> 00:25:48,640
If the order in which these locks are

817
00:25:48,640 --> 00:25:51,200
acquired -- that acquisition order --

818
00:25:51,200 --> 00:25:53,300
is not handled carefully enough,

819
00:25:53,300 --> 00:25:55,120
a deadlock problem can easily happen.

820
00:25:55,120 --> 00:25:56,380
Let's take a simple example.

821
00:25:56,380 --> 00:25:57,300
Now let's get a feel for

822
00:25:57,300 --> 00:25:58,100
what a deadlock is.

823
00:25:58,100 --> 00:25:58,960
That is to say,

824
00:25:58,960 --> 00:26:00,920
suppose we now have two locks,

825
00:26:00,920 --> 00:26:02,360
two locks, A and B.

826
00:26:02,360 --> 00:26:09,180
Now on the left there is still one execution control flow,

827
00:26:09,180 --> 00:26:11,140
and on the right there is also an execution control flow.

828
00:26:11,140 --> 00:26:14,680
So the control flow on the left,

829
00:26:14,680 --> 00:26:19,760
its order of acquiring locks is basically to acquire A first,

830
00:26:19,760 --> 00:26:21,720
and then go on to try to acquire B.

831
00:26:21,720 --> 00:26:24,740
On the right, the order is to first try to acquire B,

832
00:26:24,740 --> 00:26:26,020
and then try to acquire A.

833
00:26:26,020 --> 00:26:29,160
Now in this situation, it is possible for

834
00:26:29,160 --> 00:26:31,020
note that I said it is possible,

835
00:26:31,020 --> 00:26:32,480
meaning it is not absolutely going to happen.

836
00:26:32,480 --> 00:26:33,840
The example we are giving here,

837
00:26:33,840 --> 00:26:36,540
of course, is a state where a deadlock could possibly occur,

838
00:26:36,540 --> 00:26:38,080
it could possibly happen.

839
00:26:38,080 --> 00:26:39,240
So let's take a look at

840
00:26:39,240 --> 00:26:40,760
why this could possibly happen.

841
00:26:40,760 --> 00:26:44,560
Actually, this process is still very simple.

842
00:26:44,560 --> 00:26:45,600
Let me give you a quick demonstration.

843
00:26:45,600 --> 00:26:47,380
So, look at the left side,

844
00:26:47,380 --> 00:26:48,520
suppose this task on the left

845
00:26:48,520 --> 00:26:51,180
first gets the execution rights of the CPU.

846
00:26:51,180 --> 00:26:55,300
Then it first tries to acquire the first lock, A.

847
00:26:55,300 --> 00:26:58,040
And after it gets this lock,

848
00:26:58,040 --> 00:27:01,060
well, it

849
00:27:01,060 --> 00:27:05,420
may be in its scheduling process

850
00:27:05,420 --> 00:27:06,560
get scheduled again,

851
00:27:06,560 --> 00:27:07,840
and B gets it.

852
00:27:07,840 --> 00:27:09,660
Then B starts executing,

853
00:27:09,660 --> 00:27:11,880
since it first tries to acquire B,

854
00:27:11,880 --> 00:27:14,800
the second task

855
00:27:14,800 --> 00:27:16,500
gets the lock B.

856
00:27:16,500 --> 00:27:18,980
Now you can imagine

857
00:27:18,980 --> 00:27:22,240
that it's possible this task

858
00:27:22,240 --> 00:27:23,240
gets switched back again.

859
00:27:23,240 --> 00:27:24,220
After it's switched back,

860
00:27:24,220 --> 00:27:24,720
at this point,

861
00:27:24,720 --> 00:27:26,320
The task on the left

862
00:27:26,320 --> 00:27:27,920
when it tries to acquire B,

863
00:27:27,920 --> 00:27:31,640
then, as you can imagine, since this lock B has already been taken by the task on the right,

864
00:27:31,640 --> 00:27:33,600
already been taken by the task on the right,

865
00:27:33,600 --> 00:27:35,540
so there is no way it can get this lock.

866
00:27:35,540 --> 00:27:36,200
Right?

867
00:27:36,200 --> 00:27:38,640
So this lock is definitely not acquirable.

868
00:27:38,640 --> 00:27:40,020
Then, since it cannot be acquired,

869
00:27:40,020 --> 00:27:43,000
the task on the left is essentially stuck here,

870
00:27:43,000 --> 00:27:45,720
at this instruction, it will actually be waiting,

871
00:27:45,720 --> 00:27:47,160
waiting at this point.

872
00:27:47,160 --> 00:27:51,260
Now at this point, if the task switches back and

873
00:27:51,260 --> 00:27:52,780
B tries to acquire A,

874
00:27:52,780 --> 00:27:54,160
then B is here,

875
00:27:54,160 --> 00:27:56,260
because for A,

876
00:27:56,260 --> 00:27:58,440
it had released this lock A earlier,

877
00:27:58,440 --> 00:27:59,240
but it did not

878
00:27:59,240 --> 00:28:01,520
go ahead and acquire this lock A first,

879
00:28:01,520 --> 00:28:02,520
but it did not release it either.

880
00:28:02,520 --> 00:28:04,120
Then the lock on the right,

881
00:28:04,120 --> 00:28:06,140
the task on the right tries again,

882
00:28:06,140 --> 00:28:07,260
and maybe A naturally

883
00:28:07,260 --> 00:28:10,180
can't proceed either.

884
00:28:10,180 --> 00:28:11,480
So in this situation,

885
00:28:11,480 --> 00:28:12,500
what kind of situation arises?

886
00:28:12,500 --> 00:28:12,920
That is to say,

887
00:28:12,920 --> 00:28:15,140
if the task switches over now,

888
00:28:15,140 --> 00:28:16,740
switches to the left,

889
00:28:16,740 --> 00:28:18,480
the left task certainly can't move forward either.

890
00:28:18,480 --> 00:28:19,420
And then,

891
00:28:19,420 --> 00:28:21,020
even if it goes back to the right,

892
00:28:21,020 --> 00:28:21,960
the right can't proceed either.

893
00:28:21,960 --> 00:28:22,960
So the two tasks end up

894
00:28:22,960 --> 00:28:24,540
both waiting for each other

895
00:28:24,540 --> 00:28:25,680
to release the lock the other one holds,

896
00:28:25,680 --> 00:28:27,160
unable to make any progress.

897
00:28:27,160 --> 00:28:28,040
So under these conditions,

898
00:28:28,040 --> 00:28:29,360
this is a very typical

899
00:28:29,360 --> 00:28:31,100
deadlock situation—

900
00:28:31,100 --> 00:28:32,220
a state of deadlock.

901
00:28:32,220 --> 00:28:34,600
So as everyone can imagine,

902
00:28:34,600 --> 00:28:36,580
these two tasks

903
00:28:36,580 --> 00:28:38,460
actually have no way to proceed any further.

904
00:28:38,460 --> 00:28:40,160
This is a deadlock.

905
00:28:40,160 --> 00:28:42,700
So this

906
00:28:42,700 --> 00:28:45,560
is the simplest case

907
00:28:45,560 --> 00:28:46,460
of a deadlock occurring.

908
00:28:46,460 --> 00:28:48,860
Now, with this kind of deadlock,

909
00:28:48,860 --> 00:28:50,860
how do we solve it?

910
00:28:50,860 --> 00:28:54,760
Well, there are some principles for solving it,

911
00:28:54,760 --> 00:28:55,500
There are still some.

912
00:28:55,500 --> 00:28:56,860
and I'll briefly go over them with you.

913
00:28:56,860 --> 00:28:57,300
That is to say,

914
00:28:57,300 --> 00:28:58,840
first of all,

915
00:28:58,840 --> 00:29:01,520
we can adjust the order in which locks are acquired.

916
00:29:01,520 --> 00:29:03,940
For instance, keeping it consistent.

917
00:29:03,940 --> 00:29:04,540
What does that mean?

918
00:29:04,540 --> 00:29:05,880
Well, still taking the example above,

919
00:29:05,880 --> 00:29:06,440
you can imagine

920
00:29:06,440 --> 00:29:09,540
if the locks on both sides

921
00:29:09,540 --> 00:29:10,600
Just now, the problem we ran into,

922
00:29:10,600 --> 00:29:11,500
as you can see,

923
00:29:11,500 --> 00:29:13,000
is that in fact,

924
00:29:13,000 --> 00:29:14,240
the reason this problem occurred,

925
00:29:14,240 --> 00:29:15,040
is simply because

926
00:29:15,040 --> 00:29:17,840
the order in which they acquire locks is inconsistent.

927
00:29:17,840 --> 00:29:19,360
On the left, it's A first, then B,

928
00:29:19,360 --> 00:29:20,520
and on the right, it's B first, then A.

929
00:29:20,520 --> 00:29:23,220
So if we take this order,

930
00:29:23,220 --> 00:29:24,240
and swap it around,

931
00:29:24,240 --> 00:29:26,120
if both sides do A first, then B,

932
00:29:26,120 --> 00:29:29,680
the problem basically won't occur anymore.

933
00:29:29,680 --> 00:29:30,780
It basically won't occur anymore.

934
00:29:30,780 --> 00:29:33,000
You can go back and try this yourselves.

935
00:29:33,000 --> 00:29:36,540
It's really easy to make this comparison.

936
00:29:36,540 --> 00:29:39,920
Then the second way to solve the deadlock problem,

937
00:29:39,920 --> 00:29:43,990
is to prevent a task as much as possible

938
00:29:43,990 --> 00:29:46,190
from requesting another lock while already holding one lock.

939
00:29:46,190 --> 00:29:49,950
Now, since we saw this problem,

940
00:29:49,950 --> 00:29:52,410
one possible situation where the earlier problem exists

941
00:29:52,410 --> 00:29:54,330
So, for example, take the task on the left,

942
00:29:54,330 --> 00:29:56,370
it first acquires lock A,

943
00:29:56,370 --> 00:29:57,790
doesn't release it,

944
00:29:57,790 --> 00:30:00,190
and then goes and tries to grab another lock.

945
00:30:00,190 --> 00:30:02,970
Now that could potentially cause problems.

946
00:30:02,970 --> 00:30:03,650
See what I mean?

947
00:30:03,650 --> 00:30:07,310
So what should we do about it?

948
00:30:07,310 --> 00:30:08,770
Well, at design time,

949
00:30:08,770 --> 00:30:09,590
we can, that is to say,

950
00:30:09,590 --> 00:30:11,210
there are quite a few approaches.

951
00:30:11,210 --> 00:30:12,730
One is,

952
00:30:12,730 --> 00:30:16,970
for instance, a fairly greedy approach:

953
00:30:16,970 --> 00:30:18,170
assume we have multiple locks,

954
00:30:18,170 --> 00:30:19,110
then when I

955
00:30:19,110 --> 00:30:21,170
from a task's perspective,

956
00:30:21,170 --> 00:30:22,810
when I acquire the locks,

957
00:30:22,810 --> 00:30:24,170
I can atomically

958
00:30:24,170 --> 00:30:25,330
request all of these locks

959
00:30:25,330 --> 00:30:26,330
in one shot.

960
00:30:26,330 --> 00:30:29,210
Then in fact, if they are all

961
00:30:29,210 --> 00:30:31,210
For example, if I acquire all of these locks,

962
00:30:31,210 --> 00:30:32,530
and then, after I finish my work,

963
00:30:32,530 --> 00:30:33,690
I release all of them at once,

964
00:30:33,690 --> 00:30:35,990
then naturally, other people

965
00:30:35,990 --> 00:30:38,230
won't end up competing with anyone else,

966
00:30:38,230 --> 00:30:39,730
and this naturally avoids

967
00:30:39,730 --> 00:30:41,390
the problem of mutual deadlock.

968
00:30:41,390 --> 00:30:41,850
Think about it, isn't that right?

969
00:30:41,850 --> 00:30:43,890
But this situation

970
00:30:43,890 --> 00:30:44,710
is actually somewhat

971
00:30:44,710 --> 00:30:46,730
well, it's a rather extreme case,

972
00:30:46,730 --> 00:30:47,850
because this is a bit excessive.

973
00:30:47,850 --> 00:30:50,910
It's basically like grabbing all the resources in your hands at once,

974
00:30:50,910 --> 00:30:52,770
but you might not immediately

975
00:30:52,770 --> 00:30:54,390
be able to use them all up right away.

976
00:30:54,390 --> 00:30:56,430
So this approach isn't really appropriate either.

977
00:30:56,430 --> 00:30:58,830
So what's a better way?

978
00:30:58,830 --> 00:30:59,550
Perhaps it's to say

979
00:30:59,550 --> 00:31:00,890
what we should do is

980
00:31:00,890 --> 00:31:02,050
when we're programming,

981
00:31:02,050 --> 00:31:02,910
What you can do is this:

982
00:31:02,910 --> 00:31:05,070
whichever locks you use,

983
00:31:05,070 --> 00:31:06,870
you only request those locks,

984
00:31:06,870 --> 00:31:07,690
and then, instead of

985
00:31:07,690 --> 00:31:09,430
requesting the next lock,

986
00:31:09,430 --> 00:31:11,470
or rather, when you request a lock,

987
00:31:11,470 --> 00:31:13,610
that is essentially requesting a shared resource.

988
00:31:13,610 --> 00:31:15,730
So when you request the next shared resource,

989
00:31:15,730 --> 00:31:19,670
you should release whatever you requested before as much as possible.

990
00:31:19,670 --> 00:31:23,390
In other words, don't hold on to one thing while grabbing another.

991
00:31:23,390 --> 00:31:25,790
Doing that can also avoid this kind of

992
00:31:25,790 --> 00:31:28,730
effectively avoid this deadlock problem.

993
00:31:28,730 --> 00:31:31,490
That's the second approach to solving deadlock.

994
00:31:31,490 --> 00:31:33,990
Now the third one is, of course, more extreme:

995
00:31:33,990 --> 00:31:36,090
use locks as little as possible.

996
00:31:36,090 --> 00:31:37,850
That is, when you're programming,

997
00:31:37,850 --> 00:31:41,910
don't get carried away thinking concurrency is just so convenient,

998
00:31:41,910 --> 00:31:44,050
just because concurrency can indeed improve efficiency.

999
00:31:44,050 --> 00:31:45,310
So in practice,

1000
00:31:45,310 --> 00:31:46,110
when programming,

1001
00:31:46,110 --> 00:31:47,070
Actually, we should start from

1002
00:31:47,070 --> 00:31:48,710
the simplest angle as much as possible

1003
00:31:48,710 --> 00:31:49,650
use less of this concurrency

1004
00:31:49,650 --> 00:31:50,550
use fewer multithreads

1005
00:31:50,550 --> 00:31:52,770
then naturally we'll use fewer locks

1006
00:31:52,770 --> 00:31:53,630
and there will be less concurrency

1007
00:31:53,630 --> 00:31:55,210
and naturally it won't lead to

1008
00:31:55,210 --> 00:31:57,110
those annoying deadlock problems

1009
00:31:57,110 --> 00:31:58,910
because once deadlock problems occur

1010
00:31:58,910 --> 00:32:01,730
debugging becomes very troublesome

1011
00:32:01,730 --> 00:32:02,350
So

1012
00:32:02,350 --> 00:32:03,590
if it can be simple

1013
00:32:03,590 --> 00:32:04,250
keep it as simple as possible

1014
00:32:04,250 --> 00:32:05,210
This is also one of our main

1015
00:32:05,210 --> 00:32:05,850
programming principles

1016
00:32:05,850 --> 00:32:06,490
that is, right from the start

1017
00:32:06,490 --> 00:32:08,290
don't make the code too fancy

1018
00:32:08,290 --> 00:32:11,210
In other words, the simpler you can make it

1019
00:32:11,210 --> 00:32:12,610
just use the simplest approach

1020
00:32:12,610 --> 00:32:14,630
to solve the most complex problems

1021
00:32:14,630 --> 00:32:16,910
is the best kind of strategy

1022
00:32:16,910 --> 00:32:20,010
So these are some

1023
00:32:20,010 --> 00:32:22,030
concepts related to deadlock.

1024
00:32:22,030 --> 00:32:26,070
So next,

1025
00:32:26,070 --> 00:32:27,270
let's actually

1026
00:32:27,270 --> 00:32:28,030
take a look at

1027
00:32:28,030 --> 00:32:30,650
one kind of

1028
00:32:30,650 --> 00:32:32,930
since ultimately we need to

1029
00:32:32,930 --> 00:32:34,470
implement in code

1030
00:32:34,470 --> 00:32:36,190
these concepts of synchronization and concurrency.

1031
00:32:36,190 --> 00:32:37,510
So let's take a look at

1032
00:32:37,510 --> 00:32:39,290
the specific implementation we adopt,

1033
00:32:39,290 --> 00:32:40,390
which is the concept of

1034
00:32:40,390 --> 00:32:41,510
the spin lock.

1035
00:32:41,510 --> 00:32:43,730
In our course for now,

1036
00:32:43,730 --> 00:32:46,730
we'll use the spin lock as a representative to introduce this to you.

1037
00:32:46,730 --> 00:32:50,410
As we discussed earlier,

1038
00:32:50,410 --> 00:32:51,590
locks fall into

1039
00:32:51,590 --> 00:32:54,290
two typical types of locks:

1040
00:32:54,290 --> 00:32:55,750
one is the non-sleepable kind,

1041
00:32:55,750 --> 00:32:56,870
One type is sleepable,

1042
00:32:56,870 --> 00:32:58,870
and then there's what's called non-sleepable.

1043
00:32:58,870 --> 00:32:59,730
As I explained just now,

1044
00:32:59,730 --> 00:33:02,230
that is, when it holds this lock,

1045
00:33:02,230 --> 00:33:04,570
the task does not drop out of scheduling.

1046
00:33:04,570 --> 00:33:05,890
It still takes part in scheduling.

1047
00:33:05,890 --> 00:33:07,570
As for the so-called sleepable lock,

1048
00:33:07,570 --> 00:33:09,310
that means when we hold this lock,

1049
00:33:09,310 --> 00:33:11,650
we will take the task out of scheduling.

1050
00:33:11,650 --> 00:33:12,630
It won't take part in scheduling.

1051
00:33:12,630 --> 00:33:17,330
Now the spinlock we're talking about here,

1052
00:33:17,330 --> 00:33:20,190
it actually falls under the typical non-sleepable locks.

1053
00:33:20,190 --> 00:33:21,030
That means that

1054
00:33:21,030 --> 00:33:22,770
when we go and acquire this lock,

1055
00:33:22,770 --> 00:33:25,510
our task still takes part in scheduling.

1056
00:33:25,510 --> 00:33:27,470
It still takes part in scheduling.

1057
00:33:27,470 --> 00:33:29,010
So as for the spinlock,

1058
00:33:29,010 --> 00:33:30,450
its English name,

1059
00:33:30,450 --> 00:33:31,310
is spinlock,

1060
00:33:31,310 --> 00:33:32,070
which literally means spinning.

1061
00:33:32,070 --> 00:33:34,130
spin means to spin, like a spiral

1062
00:33:34,130 --> 00:33:35,190
That's the concept of spinning

1063
00:33:35,190 --> 00:33:37,210
So why is it called spinning?

1064
00:33:37,210 --> 00:33:39,230
Let's take a look at its actual implementation

1065
00:33:39,230 --> 00:33:41,550
It's still very typical

1066
00:33:41,550 --> 00:33:49,290
So here is a classic example of implementing a spin lock

1067
00:33:49,290 --> 00:33:50,790
A typical example

1068
00:33:50,790 --> 00:33:53,530
As you can see

1069
00:33:53,530 --> 00:33:58,770
First, we can define a struct for a spin lock

1070
00:33:58,770 --> 00:34:01,770
Inside this struct

1071
00:34:01,770 --> 00:34:06,290
we define something that essentially represents a spin lock

1072
00:34:06,290 --> 00:34:08,710
And this lock has only one member

1073
00:34:08,710 --> 00:34:10,170
which is a

1074
00:34:10,170 --> 00:34:11,770
you can think of it simply as a flag

1075
00:34:11,770 --> 00:34:13,770
If this flag, this value, is 0,

1076
00:34:13,770 --> 00:34:14,930
it means this lock

1077
00:34:14,930 --> 00:34:15,990
is in the open state

1078
00:34:15,990 --> 00:34:17,410
it is not locked

1079
00:34:17,410 --> 00:34:18,950
When the value becomes 1,

1080
00:34:18,950 --> 00:34:21,250
the lock is essentially locked

1081
00:34:21,250 --> 00:34:25,030
This is the definition of one of its structs

1082
00:34:25,030 --> 00:34:26,170
Now, the second one

1083
00:34:26,170 --> 00:34:30,130
What follows below are actually some functions for operating on this lock

1084
00:34:30,130 --> 00:34:31,410
Since we've studied data structures

1085
00:34:31,410 --> 00:34:34,370
you should find these patterns quite familiar

1086
00:34:34,370 --> 00:34:36,930
That is, you first define a struct

1087
00:34:36,930 --> 00:34:38,850
and then, around that struct, you design

1088
00:34:38,850 --> 00:34:40,150
some related operations

1089
00:34:40,150 --> 00:34:41,350
There's even a little bit of

1090
00:34:41,350 --> 00:34:42,790
object-oriented thinking here, isn't there?

1091
00:34:42,790 --> 00:34:43,930
So, this one

1092
00:34:43,930 --> 00:34:47,350
has three basic operations

1093
00:34:47,350 --> 00:34:49,190
which are basically its API

1094
00:34:49,190 --> 00:34:50,610
One of them is initialization

1095
00:34:50,610 --> 00:34:51,810
Now, for initialization,

1096
00:34:51,810 --> 00:34:52,770
we say that this lock,

1097
00:34:52,770 --> 00:34:53,870
when we initialize it, we always put it

1098
00:34:53,870 --> 00:34:55,790
in an unlocked state

1099
00:34:55,790 --> 00:34:57,010
And then,

1100
00:34:57,010 --> 00:34:58,450
as for the spin lock,

1101
00:34:58,450 --> 00:34:59,330
It actually corresponds to

1102
00:34:59,330 --> 00:35:00,310
what we talked about earlier

1103
00:35:00,310 --> 00:35:01,430
the concept of acquiring

1104
00:35:01,430 --> 00:35:05,190
that is, trying to get hold of this lock

1105
00:35:05,190 --> 00:35:07,210
trying to lock it

1106
00:35:07,210 --> 00:35:09,910
and unlock,

1107
00:35:09,910 --> 00:35:11,170
that's releasing this lock

1108
00:35:11,170 --> 00:35:12,990
trying to open this lock

1109
00:35:12,990 --> 00:35:16,490
and giving back the right to use it

1110
00:35:16,490 --> 00:35:17,610
Now,

1111
00:35:17,610 --> 00:35:19,390
the key point is

1112
00:35:19,390 --> 00:35:21,790
this so-called spinning concept

1113
00:35:21,790 --> 00:35:23,990
actually comes from the locking operation

1114
00:35:23,990 --> 00:35:25,630
in that locking operation,

1115
00:35:25,630 --> 00:35:27,010
what kind of concept do you see there?

1116
00:35:27,010 --> 00:35:28,690
it has a loop inside

1117
00:35:28,690 --> 00:35:29,610
and this loop,

1118
00:35:29,610 --> 00:35:31,570
you can see it's an infinite loop

1119
00:35:31,570 --> 00:35:32,430
So,

1120
00:35:32,430 --> 00:35:34,210
inside this infinite loop,

1121
00:35:34,210 --> 00:35:35,090
What does it do?

1122
00:35:35,090 --> 00:35:36,410
Well, once it comes in,

1123
00:35:36,410 --> 00:35:37,930
it first checks

1124
00:35:37,930 --> 00:35:40,550
whether this lock is already locked.

1125
00:35:40,550 --> 00:35:42,350
