00:53:18,340 --> 00:53:19,860
logically never interrupted.

1678
00:53:19,860 --> 00:53:23,620
So what we actually implement here is using

1679
00:53:23,620 --> 00:53:25,500
disabling interrupts to achieve this.

1680
00:53:25,500 --> 00:53:28,080
Please pay attention to this point.

1681
00:53:28,080 --> 00:53:32,600
Let's take a look at the actual code.

1682
00:53:32,600 --> 00:53:35,580
So for the code,

1683
00:53:35,580 --> 00:53:42,220
we'll go straight into our OS code.

1684
00:53:42,220 --> 00:53:44,860
Today's should correspond to our number 09.

1685
00:53:44,860 --> 00:53:46,600
It corresponds to today's number...

1686
00:53:46,600 --> 00:53:50,180
The one just now was chapter 14.

1687
00:53:50,180 --> 00:53:53,040
And here, in the examples, it's 09.

1688
00:53:53,040 --> 00:53:54,200
The 09 lock part.

1689
00:53:54,200 --> 00:53:56,820
Now, the code for this lock,

1690
00:53:56,820 --> 00:53:59,060
it's all concentrated in a single file,

1691
00:53:59,060 --> 00:54:01,780
called lock.c.

1692
00:54:01,780 --> 00:54:06,120
So this lock.c,

1693
00:54:06,120 --> 00:54:06,600
as you can see,

1694
00:54:06,600 --> 00:54:07,960
our spinlock,

1695
00:54:07,960 --> 00:54:09,100
the way it's implemented,

1696
00:54:09,100 --> 00:54:11,440
is actually very simple.

1697
00:54:11,440 --> 00:54:13,760
It just disables

1698
00:54:13,760 --> 00:54:17,820
interrupts in machine mode.

1699
00:54:17,820 --> 00:54:18,820
And what it operates on is

1700
00:54:18,820 --> 00:54:20,280
the mstatus register.

1701
00:54:20,280 --> 00:54:20,940
This register,

1702
00:54:20,940 --> 00:54:22,860
we've actually mentioned it to you before.

1703
00:54:22,860 --> 00:54:24,740
So when unlocking,

1704
00:54:24,740 --> 00:54:27,100
we simply restore and re-enable the interrupts.

1705
00:54:27,100 --> 00:54:31,500
Now, this is a very simple and brute-force approach,

1706
00:54:31,500 --> 00:54:33,380
but in this particular scenario of ours,

1707
00:54:33,380 --> 00:54:36,540
it can achieve a similar effect.

1708
00:54:36,540 --> 00:54:37,540
So let's take a look,

1709
00:54:37,540 --> 00:54:39,660
and demonstrate what happens with interrupts

1710
00:54:39,660 --> 00:54:43,120
after locking them.

1711
00:54:43,120 --> 00:54:44,940
So when doing the experiment,

1712
00:54:44,940 --> 00:54:48,220
you can look directly at our user.c.

1713
00:54:48,220 --> 00:54:48,980
In there,

1714
00:54:48,980 --> 00:54:50,620
we've defined two tasks.

1715
00:54:50,620 --> 00:54:53,240
Now in the first task,

1716
00:54:53,240 --> 00:54:56,100
we have a section that uses a spinlock.

1717
00:54:56,100 --> 00:54:58,840
We've defined a macro here

1718
00:54:58,840 --> 00:55:00,260
for whether to use it or not.

1719
00:55:00,260 --> 00:55:02,560
If we remove this macro,

1720
00:55:02,560 --> 00:55:04,040
then this lock, in effect,

1721
00:55:04,040 --> 00:55:04,680
Then we just don't add it.

1722
00:55:04,680 --> 00:55:06,820
So if this is defined,

1723
00:55:06,820 --> 00:55:07,900
we add the lock.

1724
00:55:07,900 --> 00:55:10,220
Now, what effect are we trying to achieve?

1725
00:55:10,220 --> 00:55:11,180
That is to say, as you can see,

1726
00:55:11,180 --> 00:55:12,340
we have a piece of logic like this.

1727
00:55:12,340 --> 00:55:14,200
Inside this logic,

1728
00:55:14,200 --> 00:55:16,480
in this task0,

1729
00:55:16,480 --> 00:55:18,420
after task0 prints this begin,

1730
00:55:18,420 --> 00:55:19,140
we do this.

1731
00:55:19,140 --> 00:55:21,600
We make this printing in task0,

1732
00:55:21,600 --> 00:55:23,020
execute five times.

1733
00:55:23,020 --> 00:55:24,040
Execute five times.

1734
00:55:24,040 --> 00:55:25,580
And for those five times,

1735
00:55:25,580 --> 00:55:26,980
we print a begin at the front,

1736
00:55:26,980 --> 00:55:27,800
and an end at the back.

1737
00:55:27,800 --> 00:55:29,600
So we consider this part,

1738
00:55:29,600 --> 00:55:30,560
the critical section.

1739
00:55:30,560 --> 00:55:32,600
This piece of logic is called the critical section.

1740
00:55:32,600 --> 00:55:34,400
Now this spinlock of ours,

1741
00:55:34,400 --> 00:55:35,920
At the beginning, we acquire this lock.

1742
00:55:35,920 --> 00:55:37,520
At the end, we release this lock.

1743
00:55:37,520 --> 00:55:39,340
So what effect do we achieve?

1744
00:55:39,340 --> 00:55:40,220
The idea is that we want,

1745
00:55:40,220 --> 00:55:41,380
for the code in this critical section,

1746
00:55:41,380 --> 00:55:42,900
once the lock is acquired,

1747
00:55:42,900 --> 00:55:45,160
to guarantee that the five-iteration loop below,

1748
00:55:45,160 --> 00:55:47,800
can output continuously,

1749
00:55:47,800 --> 00:55:49,760
without being interrupted by other tasks in between.

1750
00:55:49,760 --> 00:55:52,220
So what if there is no lock?

1751
00:55:52,220 --> 00:55:52,840
As you can imagine,

1752
00:55:52,840 --> 00:55:54,660
that goal cannot be achieved.

1753
00:55:54,660 --> 00:55:54,940
Right?

1754
00:55:54,940 --> 00:55:57,680
Let's remove this lock first.

1755
00:55:57,680 --> 00:56:11,550
Let's see what the effect looks like.

1756
00:56:11,550 --> 00:56:15,410
It was 09 just now, right?

1757
00:56:15,410 --> 00:56:17,230
Let's go down and compile it.

1758
00:56:17,230 --> 00:56:21,800
So right now we have no lock.

1759
00:56:21,800 --> 00:56:22,600
Without the lock,

1760
00:56:22,600 --> 00:56:24,200
what kind of effect will we see?

1761
00:56:24,200 --> 00:56:27,880
Actually, the effect is already quite obvious.

1762
00:56:27,880 --> 00:56:32,280
I just stopped it there.

1763
00:56:32,280 --> 00:56:33,540
So what can we see?

1764
00:56:33,540 --> 00:56:34,700
That is to say, task0

1765
00:56:34,700 --> 00:56:36,160
is created first.

1766
00:56:36,160 --> 00:56:37,460
task0 starts running.

1767
00:56:37,460 --> 00:56:38,440
This printing got a bit garbled.

1768
00:56:38,440 --> 00:56:40,000
Now, this running message,

1769
00:56:40,000 --> 00:56:41,620
after it was printed just once,

1770
00:56:41,620 --> 00:56:43,760
we see that as soon as an interrupt occurs,

1771
00:56:43,760 --> 00:56:46,940
the tasks actually get switched.

1772
00:56:46,940 --> 00:56:48,380
Now, because there's no lock,

1773
00:56:48,380 --> 00:56:51,680
task1 starts running.

1774
00:56:51,680 --> 00:56:54,420
So this 'running' output from task0

1775
00:56:54,420 --> 00:56:57,400
basically gets interrupted.

1776
00:56:57,400 --> 00:56:59,240
So this task0 of ours,

1777
00:56:59,240 --> 00:57:02,660
it cannot be guaranteed to output continuously.

1778
00:57:02,660 --> 00:57:04,120
We cannot guarantee continuous output.

1779
00:57:04,120 --> 00:57:06,660
And task1 will keep interrupting it in the middle.

1780
00:57:06,660 --> 00:57:09,440
So if we want to achieve this,

1781
00:57:09,440 --> 00:57:12,020
The critical section can continuously be

1782
00:57:12,020 --> 00:57:16,420
logically not interrupted.

1783
00:57:16,420 --> 00:57:17,580
Then we open this lock.

1784
00:57:17,580 --> 00:57:19,260
So based on what we just explained,

1785
00:57:19,260 --> 00:57:20,880
this actually disables interrupts inside.

1786
00:57:20,880 --> 00:57:22,000
Once interrupts are disabled,

1787
00:57:22,000 --> 00:57:23,360
it is essentially equivalent to saying that

1788
00:57:23,360 --> 00:57:24,520
this other task

1789
00:57:24,520 --> 00:57:27,040
has no chance of being scheduled at all.

1790
00:57:27,040 --> 00:57:40,290
So naturally we achieve this effect.

1791
00:57:40,290 --> 00:57:41,650
Let's take a look at whether

1792
00:57:41,650 --> 00:57:44,290
it really works exactly as we imagined.

1793
00:57:44,290 --> 00:57:47,010
As you can see, look at this Task 0.

1794
00:57:47,010 --> 00:57:49,750
Even though every time it prints

1795
00:57:49,750 --> 00:57:50,870
it gets delayed for a long time,

1796
00:57:50,870 --> 00:57:51,570
it still...

1797
00:57:51,570 --> 00:57:53,410
Task 1 at this point

1798
00:57:53,410 --> 00:57:54,670
cannot get in at all.

1799
00:57:54,670 --> 00:57:56,350
Only when this Task 0

1800
00:57:56,350 --> 00:57:57,370
has printed five times,

1801
00:57:57,370 --> 00:57:57,970
The moment it is pressed,

1802
00:57:57,970 --> 00:57:58,730
at this point,

1803
00:57:58,730 --> 00:58:00,630
because at this point,

1804
00:58:00,630 --> 00:58:01,570
we release the lock again.

1805
00:58:01,570 --> 00:58:02,330
Once the lock is released,

1806
00:58:02,330 --> 00:58:03,150
after the tasks come in,

1807
00:58:03,150 --> 00:58:04,090
Task 1

1808
00:58:04,090 --> 00:58:05,350
you can see that now they start coming in again,

1809
00:58:05,350 --> 00:58:06,190
able to get in.

1810
00:58:06,190 --> 00:58:07,770
But as soon as Task 0

1811
00:58:07,770 --> 00:58:09,190
gets scheduled in

1812
00:58:09,190 --> 00:58:09,830
and starts running,

1813
00:58:09,830 --> 00:58:10,590
then you can see

1814
00:58:10,590 --> 00:58:11,590
the other tasks

1815
00:58:11,590 --> 00:58:12,450
actually have no way

1816
00:58:12,450 --> 00:58:13,050
to interrupt it.

1817
00:58:13,050 --> 00:58:13,890
So these five iterations

1818
00:58:13,890 --> 00:58:15,490
will always run together,

1819
00:58:15,490 --> 00:58:17,030
and that is the critical section.

1820
00:58:17,030 --> 00:58:18,630
The critical section can

1821
00:58:18,630 --> 00:58:19,270
to run safely.

1822
00:58:19,270 --> 00:58:20,910
So nothing else can sneak in between.

1823
00:58:20,910 --> 00:58:22,090
Then logically,

1824
00:58:22,090 --> 00:58:23,470
we can get a certain guarantee.

1825
00:58:23,470 --> 00:58:26,250
That's the concept of synchronization.

1826
00:58:26,250 --> 00:58:29,450
This example is very simple.

1827
00:58:29,450 --> 00:58:31,730
After you go back,

1828
00:58:31,730 --> 00:58:33,430
you can try it out and feel it for yourselves.

1829
00:58:33,430 --> 00:58:37,370
So this is the spin lock.

1830
00:58:37,370 --> 00:58:39,050
The spin lock.

1831
00:58:39,050 --> 00:58:41,310
Of course, the approach we adopted

1832
00:58:41,310 --> 00:58:45,410
is really just one

1833
00:58:45,410 --> 00:58:46,630
of the ways to implement a spin lock —

1834
00:58:46,630 --> 00:58:49,650
a rather crude implementation,

1835
00:58:49,650 --> 00:58:51,330
just directly turning interrupts off.

1836
00:58:51,330 --> 00:58:52,530
We don't even spin.

1837
00:58:52,530 --> 00:58:54,350
But whether

1838
00:58:54,350 --> 00:58:55,950
it's really spinning

1839
00:58:55,950 --> 00:58:57,590
or this kind of approach,

1840
00:58:57,590 --> 00:59:00,730
when we talk about designing the use of spin locks,

1841
00:59:00,730 --> 00:59:02,110
it still has some limitations

1842
00:59:02,110 --> 00:59:03,290
of course, it has its advantages

1843
00:59:03,290 --> 00:59:04,090
the advantage is

1844
00:59:04,090 --> 00:59:08,150
its efficiency is quite high

1845
00:59:08,150 --> 00:59:10,950
because the task doesn't actually exit

1846
00:59:10,950 --> 00:59:12,990
exit the scheduling

1847
00:59:12,990 --> 00:59:14,650
so it can come back in at any time

1848
00:59:14,650 --> 00:59:16,130
now, this

1849
00:59:16,130 --> 00:59:17,790
however,

1850
00:59:17,790 --> 00:59:20,390
it also has some problems

1851
00:59:20,390 --> 00:59:21,170
as we have also seen

1852
00:59:21,170 --> 00:59:21,590
that is to say,

1853
00:59:21,590 --> 00:59:23,610
first of all, after it acquires this spinlock,

1854
00:59:23,610 --> 00:59:25,370
then, inside the critical section,

1855
00:59:25,370 --> 00:59:29,010
we actually do not recommend

1856
00:59:29,010 --> 00:59:30,910
performing any long-running operations

1857
00:59:30,910 --> 00:59:33,630
so, like what we just did

1858
00:59:33,630 --> 00:59:35,050
in that example,

1859
00:59:35,050 --> 00:59:36,310
those kinds of approaches are really not recommended

1860
00:59:36,310 --> 00:59:38,910
and in particular, that is to say

1861
00:59:38,910 --> 00:59:39,850
In normal code,

1862
00:59:39,850 --> 00:59:41,150
in the real world,

1863
00:59:41,150 --> 00:59:42,850
a spinlock is actually, because

1864
00:59:42,850 --> 00:59:44,130
of its particular properties,

1865
00:59:44,130 --> 00:59:45,710
it can indeed also be used in,

1866
00:59:45,710 --> 00:59:47,930
for example, interrupt paths.

1867
00:59:47,930 --> 00:59:49,850
So you can imagine that

1868
00:59:49,850 --> 00:59:51,310
if, in an interrupt path,

1869
00:59:51,310 --> 00:59:55,330
we were to perform some long-running operation,

1870
00:59:55,330 --> 00:59:57,610
then after using this so-called protection,

1871
00:59:57,610 --> 00:59:58,670
that effectively means

1872
00:59:58,670 --> 01:00:01,010
other tasks cannot get executed.

1873
01:00:01,010 --> 01:00:02,070
So once this interrupt

1874
01:00:02,070 --> 01:00:03,830
blocks in there for a long time,

1875
01:00:03,830 --> 01:00:05,350
that is definitely not appropriate,

1876
01:00:05,350 --> 01:00:05,970
right?

1877
01:00:05,970 --> 01:00:08,070
In particular,

1878
01:00:08,070 --> 01:00:10,450
inside a critical section protected by a spinlock,

1879
01:00:10,450 --> 01:00:11,970
you also cannot voluntarily give up the CPU.

1880
01:00:11,970 --> 01:00:13,050
That effectively means

1881
01:00:13,050 --> 01:00:16,570
This task is actually not allowed to sleep.

1882
01:00:16,570 --> 01:00:18,090
Although we didn't before...

1883
01:00:18,090 --> 01:00:19,950
we didn't demonstrate sleeping earlier.

1884
01:00:19,950 --> 01:00:20,810
But the so-called sleeping...

1885
01:00:20,810 --> 01:00:21,630
as we explained before...

1886
01:00:21,630 --> 01:00:23,850
it means you cannot give up the scheduler...

1887
01:00:23,850 --> 01:00:24,670
drop out of scheduling.

1888
01:00:24,670 --> 01:00:26,450
Now, because you can imagine...

1889
01:00:26,450 --> 01:00:27,630
that is, if you're inside a spin lock...

1890
01:00:27,630 --> 01:00:28,530
once you've acquired the lock...

1891
01:00:28,530 --> 01:00:29,950
and then you give up scheduling...

1892
01:00:29,950 --> 01:00:31,410
then it's very likely...

1893
01:00:31,410 --> 01:00:34,210
you yourself will have no way...

1894
01:00:34,210 --> 01:00:36,730
because once you voluntarily give up the CPU...

1895
01:00:36,730 --> 01:00:37,890
whether you can come back...

1896
01:00:37,890 --> 01:00:39,050
is actually questionable.

1897
01:00:39,050 --> 01:00:40,770
Suppose there's a problem in your program design...

1898
01:00:40,770 --> 01:00:42,230
and you never come back...

1899
01:00:42,230 --> 01:00:44,270
then this lock can never be released.

1900
01:00:44,270 --> 01:00:45,550
And if it can never be released...

1901
01:00:45,550 --> 01:00:48,650
Then you could end up bringing down the whole system.

1902
01:00:48,650 --> 01:00:52,130
So, that's why we say after adopting spinlocks,

1903
01:00:52,130 --> 01:00:53,870
although their advantages are quite obvious,

1904
01:00:53,870 --> 01:00:57,730
the critical sections we actually protect with spinlocks,

1905
01:00:57,730 --> 01:00:58,970
in other words,

1906
01:00:58,970 --> 01:01:01,350
you can only run some quick logic inside them.

1907
01:01:01,350 --> 01:01:03,410
Once you acquire the lock, execute very quickly,

1908
01:01:03,410 --> 01:01:04,730
and then release the lock right away.

1909
01:01:04,730 --> 01:01:08,050
You must not, inside the lock's critical section,

1910
01:01:08,050 --> 01:01:09,090
perform long-running operations,

1911
01:01:09,090 --> 01:01:10,670
or even go to sleep.

1912
01:01:10,670 --> 01:01:11,750
None of that is allowed.

1913
01:01:11,750 --> 01:01:13,150
In real systems,

1914
01:01:13,150 --> 01:01:14,090
in Linux, for example,

1915
01:01:14,090 --> 01:01:15,110
when spinlocks are used,

1916
01:01:15,110 --> 01:01:16,910
this is something we need to pay special attention to.

1917
01:01:16,910 --> 01:01:20,010
So, as for spinlocks,

1918
01:01:20,010 --> 01:01:22,170
that's all we'll cover in this brief introduction.

1919
01:01:22,170 --> 01:01:23,690
Now, this course of ours,

1920
01:01:23,690 --> 01:01:24,950
since it's fairly short,

1921
01:01:24,950 --> 01:01:27,130
Actually, the main goal is to give you a feel for

1922
01:01:27,130 --> 01:01:31,430
some basic concepts of synchronization and concurrency,

1923
01:01:31,430 --> 01:01:33,670
and the simplest use of spin locks.

1924
01:01:33,670 --> 01:01:37,170
Now, of course, there are many other synchronization techniques.

1925
01:01:37,170 --> 01:01:38,130
Here,

1926
01:01:38,130 --> 01:01:39,950
since time is limited,

1927
01:01:39,950 --> 01:01:41,730
we won't go through them one by one.

1928
01:01:41,730 --> 01:01:44,790
We've just put together a simple table for you here.

1929
01:01:44,790 --> 01:01:45,990
If you're interested,

1930
01:01:45,990 --> 01:01:47,770
there's actually a lot of material you can look at.

1931
01:01:47,770 --> 01:01:50,790
So, for example, besides spin locks,

1932
01:01:50,790 --> 01:01:53,310
we also have the most commonly used ones, such as semaphores.

1933
01:01:53,310 --> 01:01:54,790
Now, with semaphores,

1934
01:01:54,790 --> 01:01:57,870
we say they support sleeping,

1935
01:01:57,870 --> 01:02:01,250
meaning that when you hold the semaphore,

1936
01:02:01,250 --> 01:02:04,170
tasks inside your critical section are allowed to sleep.

1937
01:02:04,170 --> 01:02:06,650
Then there are also some other things, like

1938
01:02:06,650 --> 01:02:09,930
we also frequently use, for example, mutexes.

1939
01:02:09,930 --> 01:02:10,950
Now, a mutex,

1940
01:02:10,950 --> 01:02:13,350
actually, in real practice,

1941
01:02:13,350 --> 01:02:16,070
Actually, this is the simplest form of a semaphore.

1942
01:02:16,070 --> 01:02:17,250
So this...

1943
01:02:17,250 --> 01:02:22,210
There are also some others, like completion variables and so on.

1944
01:02:22,210 --> 01:02:25,610
Waking up tasks through signals, and so on.

1945
01:02:25,610 --> 01:02:28,830
So there are quite a few other synchronization techniques.

1946
01:02:28,830 --> 01:02:31,670
We won't go into them in detail here.

1947
01:02:31,670 --> 01:02:33,250
Through this chapter,

1948
01:02:33,250 --> 01:02:34,790
the main thing is to understand some basic concepts,

1949
01:02:34,790 --> 01:02:36,250
concepts related to synchronization,

1950
01:02:36,250 --> 01:02:37,710
and locking.

1951
